    "description": "Labor migration involving Iran includes foreign nationals seeking livelihoods inside the country and Iranians working abroad. Evidence on Afghan workers shows why employment, refugee protection, residence documentation and permission to work must be examined separately.",
    "tags": ["Labor migration", "Afghan migration", "Refugee livelihoods", "Employment", "Informal work"],
    "status": null,
    "editorial_status": "needs_research",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-28T04:09:51Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-28T04:09:51Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "Labor Migration and Iran: Work, Documentation and Livelihoods",
      "description": "Explore the distinction between work and migration status in Iran, with documented evidence on Afghan workers and refugee livelihood programs."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "work-and-protection",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Work and protection are different questions",
        "content": "Labor migration concerns movement connected with earning a livelihood, but a person's employment does not fully explain why they crossed a border. The evidence on Afghans in Iran includes both economic migration and forced displacement. Refugees may work, while people seeking employment may also face insecurity. An employed Afghan resident should therefore not automatically be classified as someone who migrated exclusively for work. The IMF's regional study explicitly distinguishes refugees from economic migrants while examining their overlapping livelihood difficulties. [s1]\n\nThis distinction also changes the questions a reader should ask. Employment statistics describe people working; documentation records describe administrative status; return statistics describe movement across a border. None alone measures the entire migrant labor force. In particular, a refugee population total is not a count of employed refugees, and a livelihood-program total is not a count of everyone who needs work. [s1][s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "documented-employment-restrictions",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Documentation shapes access to work",
        "content": "UNHCR's 2023 livelihood update described temporary work permits for eligible male refugee-card holders and identified agriculture, construction and furnace-related work among the main permitted sectors. It also reported that many Afghans worked as informal daily laborers. These are dated descriptions of the system, rather than a statement that the same eligibility conditions remained unchanged at the editorial cutoff. [s2]\n\nThe IMF's 2025 analysis similarly identifies occupational restrictions and difficulties entering formal employment. Its discussion links legal uncertainty and restricted opportunities with vulnerability to exploitation and discrimination. These findings help explain why having useful skills does not necessarily translate into access to an appropriate formal job. They also caution against describing all migrant employment as freely chosen participation in an unrestricted labor market. [s1]\n\nIndependent research from Migration Policy Institute emphasizes the importance of maintaining refugee documentation. Its January 2025 account describes recurring renewal requirements under Amayesh, Iran's refugee registration system, and the consequences of missing renewals. Residence security and employment opportunity must consequently be analyzed together, while remaining distinct legal questions. [s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2", "s3"]
      },
      {
        "id": "skills-and-livelihood-support",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Training is one part of a livelihood",
        "content": "UNHCR's documented interventions combine vocational education with support for wage employment and self-employment. Its partnership with Iran's Technical and Vocational Training Organization serves Afghan and Iraqi refugees. Reported activities include garment production, carpentry, shoemaking, furniture manufacturing and welding, alongside other technical fields. The program therefore extends beyond a narrow image of migrant work as construction labor. [s2]\n\nHowever, participation is an output of a program, not proof of a lasting employment outcome. A training count cannot establish subsequent wages, job security, successful business survival or freedom from exploitation. Those outcomes require follow-up evidence. Likewise, an individual business example illustrates a possibility without establishing how typical that experience is. [s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "scope-and-conclusion",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Reading Iran's labor migration carefully",
        "content": "The strongest evidence assembled here concerns migrants working inside Iran, particularly Afghans. It cannot establish the scale or occupational distribution of Iranian citizens employed abroad. That outward dimension requires destination-country labor and residence data rather than assumptions drawn from refugee livelihoods inside Iran.\n\nThe central lesson is that access to work depends on more than the availability of a job. Documentation, permitted occupations and the ability to enter formal employment all shape livelihoods in the inspected research. A fuller account must also distinguish training provision from sustained employment, and announced opportunities from demonstrated results. [s1][s2][s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2", "s3"]
      }
    ],
    "datasets": [],
    "sources": [
      {
        "id": "s1",
        "title": "Afghan Exodus: Regional Macroeconomic Implications and Policy Challenges",
        "publisher": "International Monetary Fund",
