  {
    "schema_version": "1.0",
    "page_id": "topic-10-2-6",
    "page_path": "/explore/society/migration/iranian-diaspora/",
    "name": "Iranian Diaspora",
    "level": 3,
    "parent_path": "/explore/society/migration/",
    "entity_type": "concept",
    "address": null,
    "phone": null,
    "email": null,
    "website": null,
    "logo": null,
    "contact_source_ids": [],
    "description": "The Iranian diaspora encompasses people who migrated from Iran and descendants who maintain an Iranian connection abroad. Its size depends on whether a source measures birthplace, ancestry, parental origin or citizenship, so destination-country totals require careful interpretation.",
    "tags": ["Iranian diaspora", "Ancestry", "International migration", "United States", "Canada", "Population statistics"],
    "status": null,
    "editorial_status": "needs_research",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-28T04:09:51Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-28T04:09:51Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "The Iranian Diaspora: Origins, Communities and Population Definitions",
      "description": "An evidence-based introduction to Iranian communities abroad and the differences between birthplace, ancestry and broader diaspora estimates."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "defining-diaspora",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "A diaspora is broader than an immigrant population",
        "content": "The Iranian diaspora cannot be reduced to a single count of Iranian passport holders. People born in Iran, people reporting Iranian ancestry, and children of Iranian-origin parents can fall into different statistical categories. Some descendants were born in their country of residence and have never migrated themselves. Pew Research Center's American analysis illustrates the broader approach: it includes Iranian birthplace, ancestry or race, and qualifying parental origins. [s1]\n\nThe choice of definition changes the population being described. A birthplace measure is useful for studying migration and settlement among people who moved internationally. An ancestry measure can also capture descendants. Neither automatically measures language use, cultural participation, attachment to Iran or political beliefs. Readers should therefore look for the definition accompanying a diaspora estimate before comparing it with another publication. [s1][s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "historical-pathways",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Education, displacement and settlement",
        "content": "Migration Policy Institute traces American arrivals before the revolution largely to students, professionals and tourists. The revolution of 1978–1979 and the subsequent Iran–Iraq War helped reshape migration, including arrivals from religious and ethnic minorities. Its account also describes students who remained after their studies. These pathways explain why a settled community can contain people whose original journeys had very different purposes. [s2]\n\nCanada's official heritage account likewise describes relatively small Iranian communities before migration increased following the revolution. It associates much of that migration with political, cultural or religious persecution. This is a historical account of the Canadian setting, rather than evidence that every Iranian immigrant shared the same experience or reason for leaving. [s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s2", "s3"]
      },
      {
        "id": "destination-evidence",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Two destination-country perspectives",
        "content": "Pew's analysis estimates 750,000 Iranian Americans in 2024 under its broad population definition. It uses American Community Survey information and rounds population estimates to the nearest 5,000. This is a survey-based estimate of a defined American population, not a global diaspora total. Its methodology also notes a change in identification through race in 2024, which matters when interpreting its historical comparisons. [s1]\n\nStatistics Canada's summary reports 200,465 people with Iranian ethnicity in the 2021 Census. That figure describes an ethnic-origin response, not simply people born in Iran or holding Iranian citizenship. The article places Iranian respondents within a wider account of full or partial Asian origins in Canada. Its national total should not be compared directly with the American estimate as though both agencies used the same question, population universe and year. [s4]\n\nThese examples establish substantial communities in both countries while leaving a wider geographic picture unresolved. Neither a North American estimate nor an older destination ranking establishes the distribution of the entire diaspora at the editorial cutoff. [s1][s4]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s4"]
      },
      {
        "id": "interpreting-community",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Population totals do not describe a single collective voice",
        "content": "A demographic category groups people for measurement; it does not establish agreement among them. The sources distinguish immigrants from descendants and describe different historical routes into settlement. Those distinctions are a sound starting point for studying community life, but they cannot establish what all members believe or how they relate to Iran. Claims about collective political preferences would require separate, representative evidence. [s1][s2]\n\nThe most useful conclusion is methodological as well as historical: specify who is included, where they live and when they were measured. Keeping those questions visible allows diaspora history to include descendants while preserving the distinction between ancestry and actual international movement. It also prevents incompatible national statistics from becoming an apparently precise but unsupported worldwide total. [s1][s4]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2", "s4"]
      }
    ],
